NAME
       bc - arbitrary precision calculator language

SYNOPSIS
       bc [-lq] [file...]

DESCRIPTION
       bc reads statements from the files, then from standard input,
       and prints the value of every expression statement that is not
       an assignment. Numbers are decimal and may have any number of
       digits. Interactive and piped use behave the same:

              $ echo '2^100' | bc
              1267650600228229401496703205376
              $ echo 'scale=50; 1/7' | bc
              .14285714285714285714285714285714285714285714285714

OPTIONS
       -l     Load the math library and set scale to 20.
       -q     Do not print the welcome line on a terminal.

NUMBERS AND SCALE
       scale is the number of digits kept after the decimal point by
       division, square root and powers with negative exponents. It
       starts at 0, so 10/3 is 3. Addition, subtraction and
       multiplication keep the digits their operands need.

       ibase and obase set the input and output bases (2 to 16 for
       ibase, 2 to 999 for obase). Input digits above 9 are the
       capital letters A to F. Results longer than 70 characters are
       split over several lines ending in a backslash.

EXPRESSIONS
       From lowest to highest precedence:

       ||              logical or
       &&              logical and
       !               logical not
       == != < <= > >= comparison, 1 or 0
       = += -= *= /= %= ^=   assignment (right to left)
       + -             addition, subtraction
       * / %           multiplication, division, remainder
       ^               power (integer exponent, right to left)
       -               negation
       ++ --           increment, decrement (prefix or postfix)

       Variables and function names are lower case letters, digits
       and _. Arrays are written name[index]. The special variables
       are scale, ibase, obase and last (also written .), which holds
       the last printed value.

       Builtin functions: sqrt(x), length(x) (significant digits),
       scale(x) (digits after the point), read() (a number from
       standard input).

STATEMENTS
       expression        printed unless it is an assignment
       "string"          printed as is
       print list        prints expressions and strings, \n escapes
       { statements }
       if (cond) statement [else statement]
       while (cond) statement
       for (init; cond; step) statement
       break, continue
       define name(params) { auto locals; statements }
       return, return (expression)
       quit              leave as soon as it is read
       halt              leave when it is executed
       limits            print the implementation limits

       Statements end with a newline or ;. Comments are /* ... */ and
       # to the end of the line.

MATH LIBRARY
       With -l: e(x) exponential, l(x) natural logarithm, s(x) sine,
       c(x) cosine, a(x) arctangent (radians).

              $ echo '4*a(1)' | bc -l
              3.14159265358979323844

EXAMPLES
       Factorial with a function:

              define f(n) {
                if (n <= 1) return (1)
                return (n * f(n - 1))
              }
              f(30)

       Hexadecimal:   echo 'obase=16; 255' | bc      prints FF

SEE ALSO
       sh(1)

NOTES
       The arithmetic is written from scratch for UC.OS: one decimal
       digit per byte and schoolbook algorithms, so very large
       divisions are slow.

